From: Martin Pluskal <mpluskal@suse.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cmake: drop /usr/include from LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS

LLVM distributions that install their headers directly into /usr/include
(e.g. openSUSE Factory, -- Found LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS=/usr/include) end up
passing it to the compiler as -isystem via include_directories(SYSTEM ...)
and TVM_LLVM_INCLUDE_FLAGS below. An explicitly listed -isystem directory
takes the compiler default's place in the search order, which breaks GCC's
#include_next lookup (c++/cstdlib -> stdlib.h: No such file or directory)
for every C++ translation unit that includes a C++ standard header.

/usr/include is on the compiler's default search path anyway, so filter it
out; LLVM headers in /usr/include/llvm* keep resolving as before.

--- a/cmake/modules/LLVM.cmake
+++ b/cmake/modules/LLVM.cmake
@@ -32,6 +32,12 @@
   if (${TVM_LLVM_VERSION} LESS 150)
     message(FATAL_ERROR "LLVM version 15.0 or greater is required.")
   endif()
+  # LLVM installations with headers directly in /usr/include list it in
+  # LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS. Passing it via -isystem breaks GCC's #include_next
+  # (e.g. cstdlib -> stdlib.h not found), and it is searched by default
+  # anyway, so drop it here. LLVM headers under /usr/include/llvm* still
+  # resolve through the default search path.
+  list(REMOVE_ITEM LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS "/usr/include")
   include_directories(SYSTEM ${LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS})
   add_definitions(${LLVM_DEFINITIONS})
   add_library(tvm_llvm_header INTERFACE)
